{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: the model is generally too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift (≈ -0.5 to -2 EUR early horizon; ≈ -3 to -10 EUR by days 15–30 depending on trend).",
    "Underestimates downside momentum: when the recent history is trending down, the model mean-reverts upward too quickly. Enforce stronger continuation of the prevailing downtrend for the next ~2–3 weeks (reduce the rebound slope).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: forecast error grows with horizon (small near-term, large long-term). Use an increasing correction with lead time (e.g., subtract an additional ~0.2–0.4 EUR per forecast day after day 10, capped).",
    "Overshoot on rebounds after drawdowns: after a sharp drop in history, the model projects a recovery to mid-70s that does not materialize. Cap rebound magnitude using recent realized volatility/range (e.g., limit upside to last price + 0.5×(10-day ATR) over the next 30d).",
    "Peak/trough timing lag: the model’s local highs occur too early and too high; realized path turns down sooner and deeper. Shift turning points later and damp amplitudes (reduce oscillation/seasonal component)."
  ]
}
